# Keep secrets OUT of the image.
#
# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .` and there was no .dockerignore, so every env
# file in backend/ was baked into brio-pm-api:latest. As of 2026-09-10 that image
# contained FOUR:
#
#   /app/.env                      ~16 vars of live credentials
#   /app/.env.bak
#   /app/.env.bak-1778794467
#   /app/.env.bak.1778960402
#
# Both deploy-api-1 and deploy-worker-1 run this image, and brio-pm holds client
# estimates, invoices and documents. Rotating a credential on the host never
# removed it from the image, and the backups meant historical values persisted
# too — including ones already rotated for security reasons.
#
# Found 2026-09-10 while fixing the same defect in another internal app, where it caused a
# real failure rather than a theoretical one: a secret was cleared on the host,
# the container recreated, `docker exec ... echo $VAR` correctly reported empty,
# and the application kept reading the baked copy, because pydantic-settings
# loads `.env` as a FILE. The host said one thing and the app read another, in
# the middle of an outage.
#
# Nothing excluded here is needed at runtime: deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml
# passes `env_file: ../backend/.env` to both api and worker, so the values arrive
# as environment variables, which pydantic reads at higher priority than a file.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local development artifacts. .venv would add hundreds of MB and shadow the
# packages the build installs.
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Scratch and generated output that has no business in a runtime image.
scratchpad/
*.log

# tests/ is deliberately NOT excluded — the suite is run inside the runtime image
# against a throwaway database, never against production.
